<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Who Oli's Bookshelf is for, and the editorial standards that keep reading-order guides honest."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">About the desk</p>
  <h1>A small site for a large TBR</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Oli's Bookshelf exists because long series punish first-time readers: prequels that spoil,
    novellas that look skippable, and “complete” lists that quietly include picture books or RPG
    extras.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What we publish</h2>
    <p>
      Series reading-order guides and short “what to read next” notes for science fiction, fantasy,
      and mystery/thriller long-tails. We start with publication order, then explain where chronology
      diverges. We do not rewrite novels or quote copyrighted prose.
    </p>

    <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Facts over flavor text.</strong> Titles, publication years, and “this story sits
        before that novel” claims need a source. Author and publisher pages beat fandom wikis.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No scraped blurbs.</strong> We will not paste jacket copy. If a retailer blurb
        spoils, we say so without repeating the spoiler.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drafts stay labeled.</strong> New guides ship as <code>status: draft</code> until a
        person checks the order table and the review flags.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Next-reads are arguments, not rankings.</strong> Each suggestion needs an original
        reason (“same institutional voice,” “same found-family aftercare”), not a star average.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No fake reviews.</strong> We do not invent quotations, Goodreads voices, or “I cried
        on page 400.”
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Money stays honest.</strong> Amazon Associates is not active. Search URLs use
        <code>TODO_AFFILIATE_TAG</code> only. See the <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The longer house rules live in
      <code>docs/EDITORIAL_STANDARDS.md</code> in the repository. Workflow and backlog live beside
      them.
    </p>

    <h2>Who this is not for</h2>
    <p>
      If you want recaps, leaked manuscripts, or “make money with book blogs,” this is the wrong
      shelf. We also skip the most over-covered primaries (Harry Potter, The Lord of the Rings,
      A Song of Ice and Fire, Star Wars) so the work stays on series that still confuse people.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
